    "provenance": {
     "source": "eyebiomech fem",
     "citation": "Elastic corneoscleral FEM (no remodeling; spec §7). The stress-free geometry is found by a fixed-point inverse solve so that the IOP-only state is the document's geometry, and the result is the change from that state (--reference measured instead takes the document as stress-free). Stiffness calibrated to the in-vivo ocular rigidity (data/materials). The loads other than IOP act as a linear perturbation of the pressurized eye (the nonlinear solve softens under the full muscle load, an artifact of the support model). Approximations: fixed optic-nerve support; muscle forces from length-proxy activation x capacity, brought into static equilibrium with a linear passive orbital moment by the least change, with the net force and the passive moment taken by an orbital reaction on the orbit's posterior hemisphere; lid bands fixed in the orbit at the lid margins; dead loads in the reference configuration; Zernike maps fitted within 4 mm of the axis; lens follows the scleral spur, the cornea is measured relative to the spur ring's lateral shift and tilt, and the retina follows the edge of the optic-nerve support. Materials, thickness, eyelid, and force data are unverified (see data/materials, data/eyelid, data/eom). Tests the hypothesis that muscle and lid loading shape the cornea; not an established result.",
